"events\\.csv"], ["Bash", "ls -la && wc -l events.csv && head -15 events.csv"], ["Bash", "cat README.md && cat -A events.csv | head"]], "final_message": "The most likely cause is that the `ts` column mixes three timestamp formats. `events.csv` has only three rows:\n\n```\nid,ts\n1,2026-03-01T10:00:00Z          \u2190 UTC (explicit \"Z\")\n2,2026-03-01T10:05:00           \u2190 no zone at all (naive)\n3,2026-03-01T04:10:00-06:00     \u2190 UTC-6 offset\n```\n\nMost charting tools handle these differently:\n\n- **Row 1** is unambiguous: 10:00 UTC.\n- **Row 3** is also unambiguous. 04:10 at -06:00 is 10:10 UTC, so rows 1\u20133 are really 10 minutes apart.\n- **Row 2** has no zone, so each tool guesses. pandas leaves it naive. JavaScript's `Date` and many BI tools read it as local time. Some databases assume UTC. If it's read as local time, it lands hours away from its neighbors, depending on the viewer's zone.\n\nIf you parse the column naively, or strip the offset and keep the wall-clock part, row 3 shows up as 04:10 instead of 10:10. That's a 6-hour jump. Row 2 can shift by your own UTC offset on top of that. Because the shift depends on the viewer's zone, the same data can look different on different machines.\n\n**Fix:**\n1. Find out what zone the naive timestamps are in. Check whichever system exports the file. I can't tell from the file itself, and I'm guessing that row 2 is UTC.\n2. Normalize everything to UTC on load. In pandas that's `pd.to_datetime(df.ts, utc=True)` once the naive rows are localized to the right zone.\n3. Better still, fix the exporter so it always writes an explicit offset or `Z`.\n\nI only have three rows, so I can't confirm this is the whole story for your real charts. If the full export has more rows, send it over or point me at the charting code. I can check which parser is being used and whether any other cause is involved, such as bucketing by local-time hour or DST transitions.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after"